# Project context for AI agents

> Generated by Seed4J (`ai-context` module). Adapt it to your project: it is yours from now on.

## The project

- Name: {{projectName}}
- Base package: `{{packageName}}`
- Architecture: hexagonal, one bounded context per business area.

## Who does what

| Seed4J (structure) | You, the agent (business) |
| --- | --- |
| Build, configuration, infrastructure wiring, technical modules (database, messaging, security, documentation…) | Business contexts: domain model, use cases, ports and adapters, and their tests |
| Applied with Seed4J modules, see the `seed4j-cli` skill (install it with `seed4j skill install`) | Written by you, see [`hexagonal-feature`](skills/hexagonal-feature/SKILL.md) and [`tdd-domain`](skills/tdd-domain/SKILL.md) |

Seed4J never generates business code. Don't hand-write what a Seed4J module provides (build plugins, database or messaging setup…): apply the module instead.

## Packages

```
{{packageName}}.<context>/
  package-info.java                  @{{packageName}}.BusinessContext
  domain/                            business code, no framework
    <aggregate>/                     one sub-package per aggregate when the context has several
  application/                       <Aggregates>ApplicationService, orchestration only
  infrastructure/
    primary/                         REST resources, Rest* records, scheduled jobs, error handler
    secondary/<aggregate>/           repositories and other port implementations
{{packageName}}.shared.<kernel>/     shared kernels, package-info annotated @{{packageName}}.SharedKernel
{{packageName}}.wire.<topic>/        technical wiring (Clock, scheduling…), package-info annotated @{{packageName}}.BusinessContext
```

Allowed dependencies (enforced by `HexagonalArchTest` when the `java-archunit` module is applied):

- `domain` → JDK, the same context's `domain`, shared kernels, `org.apache.commons`, `org.jmolecules`, `org.slf4j`. No Spring, no Jackson, no JPA.
- `application` → `domain` (+ Spring `@Service`, transactions, security). Never `infrastructure`.
- `infrastructure.primary` → `application`, `domain`. Never `secondary`. Controllers are not public.
- `infrastructure.secondary` → `domain`. Never `application`, never its own context's `primary`.
- A context never uses another context's `domain`: share through a `@SharedKernel` package if needed.

## Naming

| Element | Name | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Aggregate | business noun | `Loan` |
| Identity | `<Aggregate>Id` record wrapping a `UUID`, with `newId()` and `get()` | `LoanId` |
| Creation input | `<Aggregate>ToCreate` record with a `create()` method | `MemberToCreate` |
| Repository port | `<Aggregates>Repository` (plural), methods in business terms | `LoansRepository.activeLoansOf(MemberId)` |
| Domain service | noun built from a verb | `BooksBorrower`, `LoansReturner` |
| Domain exception | what went wrong | `UnknownLoanException`, `BorrowingLimitReachedException` |
| Application service | `<Aggregates>ApplicationService` | `LoansApplicationService` |
| REST resource | `<Aggregates>Resource`, package-private | `LoansResource` |
| REST DTO | `Rest<Name>` record with `from(domain)` / `toDomain()` | `RestLoan`, `RestLoanToCreate` |
| In-memory adapter | `InMemory<Aggregates>Repository`, package-private, `@Repository` | `InMemoryLoansRepository` |

Names in code are in English, ASCII only, even when the need is written in another language.

## Domain

**Value objects** are records validated in their compact constructor with `{{packageName}}.shared.error.domain.Assert`, exposing `get()` for single values. Use one for every business concept instead of `String` or `int`. The generated `Assert` has no pattern check: check formats explicitly and throw a domain exception.

```java
public record Isbn(String isbn) {
  private static final Pattern FORMAT = Pattern.compile("^\\d{13}$");

  public Isbn {
    Assert.notBlank("isbn", isbn);

    if (!FORMAT.matcher(isbn).matches()) {
      throw new InvalidIsbnException(isbn);
    }
  }

  public String get() {
    return isbn();
  }
}
```

Money is a record wrapping a `BigDecimal` with a fixed scale and `RoundingMode.HALF_UP`, never a `double`.

**Aggregates** are `final` classes with a private constructor taking a builder, mandatory fields checked with `Assert`, accessors without `get` prefix and no setters. State changes go through business methods that enforce the rules. Another aggregate is referenced by its id only.

**Domain services** orchestrate several aggregates for one business operation. Check every rule before changing any aggregate: repositories (in-memory ones in particular) may hold the very instances you modify, so a rule failing after a first change would leave the system half-updated.

**Time**: never call `now()` in the domain. Pass the current date or time as a parameter; application services get it from an injected `java.time.Clock`. If there is no `Clock` bean, declare one in `{{packageName}}.wire.clock.infrastructure.primary`.

**Errors**: give each context three abstract exception bases in its `domain` package (unknown element, business rule violation, invalid value) and make every domain exception extend one of them.

## Application

```java
@Service
public class LoansApplicationService {

  private final BooksBorrower borrower;
  private final Clock clock;

  public LoansApplicationService(MembersRepository members, BooksRepository books, LoansRepository loans, Clock clock) {
    borrower = new BooksBorrower(members, books, loans);
    this.clock = clock;
  }

  public Loan borrow(MemberId member, Isbn book) {
    return borrower.borrow(member, book, LocalDate.now(clock));
  }
}
```

Application services only orchestrate (load, call the domain, save, notify). Domain services are created by them, not declared as Spring beans.

## Adapters

- **REST**: package-private `<Aggregates>Resource` and `Rest*` records converting at the boundary; domain types never appear in JSON. One `@ControllerAdvice(basePackageClasses = <Context>ErrorsHandler.class)` per context maps its exceptions: unknown element → 404, business rule violation → 409, invalid value and `AssertionException` → 400.
- **Scheduled jobs** are primary adapters (`@Component` with `@Scheduled`) calling an application service. Enable scheduling once, in `{{packageName}}.wire.scheduling.infrastructure.primary`.
- **Repositories**: an in-memory adapter until a persistence module is applied.
- **Outgoing notifications** (email, SMS…) are domain ports; start with an adapter that logs the message.

## Quality gates

Run `./mvnw clean verify` (or `./gradlew build`) before saying a task is done. It runs the unit and integration tests, the ArchUnit rules and, depending on the applied modules, Checkstyle and the coverage check. With `jacoco-with-min-coverage-check`, every class needs 100 % line and branch coverage. Never disable or weaken a test, a rule or a check to make the build pass: ask the user instead.

